==\n-rw-r--r--. 1 scheschb amazon  440 15:14 c_src/CMakeLists.txt\n-rw-r--r--. 1 scheschb amazon  462 15:14 c_src/include/lib.h\n-rw-r--r--. 1 scheschb amazon 1661 15:14 c_src/src/lib.c\n\n=== Cargo.toml ===\n[package]\nname = \"translated_rust\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2024\"\n\n[lib]\nname = \"update_md5_lib\"\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n# This crate is a faithful, byte-for-byte translation of a C library and is\n# consumed through its C ABI.  The C code has no language-level safety net: it\n# dereferences whatever pointer it is handed (a NULL argument must fault with\n# SIGSEGV, exactly as the C does) and it relies on unsigned wraparound\n# throughout.  rustc's dev-profile UB checks would instead turn a NULL\n# dereference into a Rust panic/abort (SIGABRT), which is an observable\n# divergence from the C at the FFI boundary, so they are disabled here to keep\n# the `dev` build's behaviour identical to `release` and to the C.\n# All arithmetic in `src/lib.rs` already uses explicit `wrapping_*`, so\n# `overflow-checks` is a no-op for correctness and is disabled for the same\n# reason.\n[profile.dev]\ndebug-assertions = false\noverflow-checks = false\n\n[profile.release]\npanic = \"abort\"\n\n[workspace]\n\n=== calls into Rust go ONLY via libloading (no direct linkage) ===\n  no direct crate linkage anywhere in tests/\ntests/harness/mod.rs:0\ntests/phase_d_symbols.rs:0\ntests/phase_b_configs.rs:0\ntests/phase_c_errors.rs:0\ntests/harness/mod.rs:12\ntests/phase_b_configs.rs:10\ntests/phase_c_errors.rs:7","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_xl3tkdcu7hbmh2medxk5tv4sql2euvf6ubxjpfdt5qckridk3ana","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/src/lib.c` is one translation unit exporting three symbols (only `update_md5` is in the public header; the other two have external linkage and are therefore part of the `.so` ABI, so I drove them directly too):\n\n| symbol | role |\n|---|---|\n| `tflac_pack_u64le` | writes a `u64` little-endian into 8 bytes |\n| `tflac_md5_addsample` | all the branching: `pos % 64`, `if (pos >= 64)`, `while (bytes--)` copy loop |\n| `update_md5` | 5 fixed iterations, `bits = 64`, `samples += 32` elements, returns `cur_blocksize*channels - 40` |\n\n## Artifacts created (crate root)\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — 3/3 C symbols exported by the Rust `.so`, exact names; `nm -D` diff **empty**; 0 unresolved non-libc imports; struct layout parity confirmed against the C compiler (`tflac_md5` = 88/8, `pos`@0 `total`@8 `buffer`@16; `tflac` = 96/8, `cur_blocksize`@88 `channels`@92).\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 20 rows, derived by grepping the C: it has **1** `return`, **1** `if`, **0** asserts/null checks/error codes. Rows cover null pointers, `bits==0`, non-multiples of 8, `u32::MAX`, `pos` out of range, `pos`/`total` wraparound, the write spilling into `buffer[64..72]`, the copy loop reading out to `buffer[126]`, zero-iteration `while (bytes--)`, `b` underflow/overflow, and the short-`samples` OOB read.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 32 rows over 12 mechanically-derived axes, including full sweeps (`pos` 0..63, `b` 1..80, `d` at every arena offset), streaming/chained state, a mixed-pipeline fuzz across all three entry points, and a global fuzz.\n- **`tests/`** — `harness/mod.rs` + `phase_b_configs.rs` (33 tests) + `phase_c_errors.rs` (18) + `phase_d_symbols.rs` (4). Both `.so`s are `dlopen`ed via `libloading`; the Rust crate is never linked or called directly.\n- **`run_all.sh`** — enumerates feature combos, `cargo check`s each, builds C + both Rust profiles, diffs `nm -D`, runs everything.\n\nKey technique: since the C reads out of bounds by design, each side runs over its own copy of the same seeded 4 KiB arena, and the whole arena plus the return value are compared byte-for-byte — making the UB deterministic and comparable.\n\n## Two real problems found\n\n1. **The test suite was silently vacuous.** `cargo test` does *not* rebuild a `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` target, because integration tests `dlopen` it rather than link it. My first mutation run proved this: 15 deliberately injected bugs in `src/lib.rs` were **all** undetected and the suite reported a clean pass. Fixed with a staleness guard in the harness (panics if the `.so` is older than `src/*.rs`) plus an explicit `cargo build` in `run_all.sh`. After the fix, **19/19 genuine code mutations are caught** (byte order, shift amounts, `% 64`→`% 63`, `>= 64`→`> 64`, `bits/8`→`bits/4`, copy-loop off-by-one, `i <= 4`→`i < 4`, the 32-element stride, `b -= step`→`+=`, `cb*ch`→`cb+ch`, …).\n\n2. **A dev-profile-only divergence on `NULL`.** rustc's UB check turned the null store in `tflac_pack_u64le` into a Rust panic → `SIGABRT` (6), while the C faults with `SIGSEGV` (11). Release already matched. Since this crate is consumed purely through its C ABI and a C library has no such check, I disabled `debug-assertions`/`overflow-checks` for `[profile.dev]` (documented inline); dev now matches release and C. The test compares the actual child-process *signal* per side.\n\n**`src/lib.rs` required no changes** — the translation was already byte-for-byte faithful. Only `Cargo.toml` was edited (`libloading` dev-dep + the profile note). Nothing in `c_src/` was modified. As an extra robustness check, the Rust also matches a `-O2` C build (built outside `c_src/`), so the C's UB isn't being exploited differently across optimization levels."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2074,"cache_read_input_tokens":149320,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2074,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"936b14bf-25ea-4f7b-b71f-732e46a1b101","uuid":"ef81c2f6-f442-4b21-8457-dc07b65e4630","timestamp":"2026-08-19T15:38:00.172Z"}
